Fix a wild free() in the Quickshell PAM subprocess that turns any IPC write
failure into a SIGSEGV.

PamSubprocess::conversation()'s `fail:` label runs `free(responseArray)`.
`responseArray` is the `pam_response**` OUT-PARAM handed in by the PAM module,
not the buffer this function allocated. Modules pass the address of a local --
pam_fprintd's send_msg() does `struct pam_response *resp; pc->conv(1, &msgp,
&resp, pc->appdata_ptr);` -- so this frees a stack address. The buffer that
should be freed is `responses`, the calloc() at the top of the function.

Under jemalloc (Quickshell's default) the invalid free segfaults inside
je_free_default; under glibc it aborts with "free(): invalid pointer". Either
way the crash handler runs and files a Quickshell crash report whose backtrace
looks like the shell itself crashed -- the subprocess is a fork(), so every
frame below PamConversation::createSubprocess is the parent's inherited stack,
right down to qs::launch::main.

The `fail:` path is reached whenever a writeBytes()/writeString() to the parent
fails, which is exactly what happens when the shell dies while a PAM child is
still blocked in a slow module (e.g. pam_fprintd's ~30s verify): the next
conversation message hits EPIPE.

Applies to Quickshell v0.3.0; unfixed on master as of 28771c7.

Second hunk: add O_CLOEXEC to the IPC pipe creation in
PamConversation::createSubprocess(). The pipe() calls at the top of that
function have no O_CLOEXEC, so both the fds the parent keeps and the fds the
PAM worker keeps survive any execve() in either process. The worker's own
crash handler (src/crash/handler.cpp) forks and execve()s a separate crash
reporter binary on a fatal signal; without O_CLOEXEC that reporter inherits
the worker's write end of the IPC pipe. As long as the reporter process is
alive it holds that write end open, so the parent's read() on its end never
returns EOF and PamConversation::onMessage() never fires -- the shell hangs
on "authenticating" forever even though the worker is dead.

The parent already turns a genuine EOF into a completion: PamIpcPipes::
readBytes() treats a 0-byte read() as failure, onMessage()'s `fail:` label
calls PamConversation::internalError(), which emits error(InternalError),
which PamContext::onError() turns into completed(PamResult::Error) for QML's
onCompleted. So O_CLOEXEC alone is sufficient here: it lets the crash
reporter exec without inheriting the pipe, restoring a real EOF that the
existing parent-side logic already converts into a completion. No dup2 is
needed -- neither the PAM worker (which stores its fds as PamSubprocess
constructor arguments) nor the crash reporter (which receives its own fds
by dup()ing and passing them through env vars) depend on these pipes
keeping specific fd numbers.

Uses pipe2() for atomicity (no fork-then-fcntl race). fcntl.h is added to
the includes for O_CLOEXEC; pipe2() is declared by unistd.h, already
included above.
---
diff --git a/src/services/pam/subprocess.cpp b/src/services/pam/subprocess.cpp
--- a/src/services/pam/subprocess.cpp
+++ b/src/services/pam/subprocess.cpp
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 #include "subprocess.hpp"
 #include <array>
+#include <fcntl.h>
 #include <ostream>
 #include <string>
@@ -27,7 +28,8 @@ pid_t PamConversation::createSubprocess(
 	auto toSubprocess = std::array<int, 2>();
 	auto fromSubprocess = std::array<int, 2>();

-	if (pipe(toSubprocess.data()) == -1 || pipe(fromSubprocess.data()) == -1) {
+	if (pipe2(toSubprocess.data(), O_CLOEXEC) == -1
+	    || pipe2(fromSubprocess.data(), O_CLOEXEC) == -1) {
 		qCDebug(logPam) << "Failed to create pipes for subprocess.";
 		return 0;
 	}
@@ -201,6 +203,6 @@ int PamSubprocess::conversation(
 	return PAM_SUCCESS;

 fail:
-	free(responseArray); // NOLINT
+	free(responses); // NOLINT
 	_exit(1);
 }
